{"name":"Artemis Silver","hex":"#ddddee","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"silver","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A lustrous, white, metallic element, atomic number 47, atomic weight 107.87, symbol Ag.","Coins made from silver or any similar white metal."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made from silver.","Made from another white metal."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/silver"}],"entries":[{"title":"Artemis","blocks":[{"text":"In ancient Greek religion and mythology, Artemis is the goddess of hunting, the wilderness, wild animals, transitions, nature, vegetation, childbirth, care of children, and chastity. She was often said to roam the forests and mountains, attended by an entourage of nymphs. The goddess Diana is her Roman equivalent."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Diane_de_Versailles_-_Mus%C3%A9e_du_Louvre_AGER_Ma_589.jpg/330px-Diane_de_Versailles_-_Mus%C3%A9e_du_Louvre_AGER_Ma_589.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artemis","wikidata":"Q39503"},{"title":"Silver","blocks":[{"text":"Silver is a chemical element; it has symbol Ag and atomic number 47. A soft, white, lustrous transition metal, it exhibits the highest electrical conductivity, thermal conductivity and reflectivity of any metal. Silver is found in the Earth's crust in the pure, free elemental form, as an alloy with gold (electrum) and other metals, and in minerals such as acanthite, argentite and chlorargyrite. Most silver is recovered as a byproduct of lead–zinc, copper and gold mining and refining."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Silver_crystal.jpg/330px-Silver_crystal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver","wikidata":"Q1090"},{"title":"Silver (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Silver or metallic gray is a color tone resembling gray that is a representation of the color of polished silver."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Clemens_Friedell_silver_centerpiece_bowl_top_view_-_HLABG.png/330px-Clemens_Friedell_silver_centerpiece_bowl_top_view_-_HLABG.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_(color)","wikidata":"Q317802"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/0_Art%C3%A9mis_%28Diane%29_-_Galleria_dei_Candelabri_-_Vatican.JPG/500px-0_Art%C3%A9mis_%28Diane%29_-_Galleria_dei_Candelabri_-_Vatican.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/0_Art%C3%A9mis_%28Diane%29_-_Galleria_dei_Candelabri_-_Vatican.JPG/1280px-0_Art%C3%A9mis_%28Diane%29_-_Galleria_dei_Candelabri_-_Vatican.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/0_Art%C3%A9mis_%28Diane%29_-_Galleria_dei_Candelabri_-_Vatican.JPG/500px-0_Art%C3%A9mis_%28Diane%29_-_Galleria_dei_Candelabri_-_Vatican.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Artémis (Diane), the huntress. Roman copy of a Greek statue, 2nd century. Galleria dei Candelabri – Vatican Museums","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A0_Art%C3%A9mis_(Diane)_-_Galleria_dei_Candelabri_-_Vatican.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Artemis%2C_Parian_pottery_plate%2C_675-600_BC%2C_AM_Mykonos%2C_177233.jpg/500px-Artemis%2C_Parian_pottery_plate%2C_675-600_BC%2C_AM_Mykonos%2C_177233.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Artemis%2C_Parian_pottery_plate%2C_675-600_BC%2C_AM_Mykonos%2C_177233.jpg/1280px-Artemis%2C_Parian_pottery_plate%2C_675-600_BC%2C_AM_Mykonos%2C_177233.jpg?utm_s